  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 154 AM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nToday there is a weak upper-level ridge over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will allow skies to clear. As a \nresult of this combined with snow melting yesterday, fog is possible \nacross portions of North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana \nthis morning. See the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios section for \ndetails. It will warm up a few degrees today across North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana but still remains well-below \nseasonal averages. It will be dry today across the area. On \nWednesday the weak upper-level ridge remains over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This allows the potential for \nthere to be fog across North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana tonight into Wednesday morning. See the Forecast Confidence \n& Scenarios section for details. On Wednesday temperatures will warm \nup some more but remain below seasonal averages. It will be dry \nacross the area on Wednesday. \n\nOn Thursday an upper-level trough briefly moves over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. On Thursday temperatures will be \nnear seasonal averages with dry weather. On Friday and Saturday an \nupper-level ridge moves over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This brings warm temperatures and dry weather \nto the area on Friday and Saturday. On Friday and Saturday \ntemperatures will be above seasonal averages. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThere is a 60% chance for dense fog (visibility less than one half \nmile) in most areas of the plains of North-central Montana and most \nof the Central and Southwestern Montana valleys through this \nmorning. From 12 PM to 6 PM today there is a 40 to 60% chance for \ndense fog (visibility less than one half mile) in the Gallatin and \nHelena Valleys. Tuesday night into Wednesday morning there is a 30 - \n50% chance for fog in most valleys of Southwestern and Central \nMontana and across most of the plains of North-central Montana. The \nbiggest area of uncertainty with fog this morning is if skies will \nclear and if winds will remain light. In areas that clear and have \nlight winds dense fog will form. As a result across the plains of \nNorth-central Montana and most of the valleys of Central and \nSouthwestern Montana a Dense Fog Advisory was issued through Noon \ntoday. For fog Tuesday night into Wednesday morning the biggest area \nof uncertainty is if snow will remain on grassy surfaces to enhance \nfog development. In areas that snow remains dense fog will have at \nleast a moderate chance of forming Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning due to calm winds and clear skies. -IG",
